\section{Methods that delegate to the final model}{
\code{coef()}, \code{fitted()}, \code{residuals()}, \code{nobs()}, \code{formula()}, \code{family()},
\code{deviance()}, and \code{df.residual()} call the same generic on the final
model, so their results, and their errors, are the native ones. For
example, \code{unmarked} fits support \code{coef()}, \code{fitted()}, and \code{residuals()}
through the \code{unmarked} methods, while \code{family()} stops with the native
error. Coefficient standard errors from \code{summary(fit$opt_mod)} or
\code{vcov(fit$opt_mod)} condition on the estimated scales; see
\code{\link[=vcov.multiScaleR]{vcov.multiScaleR()}} for the joint covariance of the analytic backends.
}

\section{Log-likelihood and information criteria}{
\code{logLik()} returns the maximized log-likelihood of the final model. Its
\code{df} attribute counts the native model parameters plus each estimated
scale (sigma) and each estimated kernel shape, exactly as \code{K} in
\code{\link[=aic_tab]{aic_tab()}}. \code{AIC(fit)} therefore equals the AIC reported by
\code{aic_tab(list(fit), AICc = FALSE)}, and \code{BIC(fit)} equals the BIC from
\code{\link[=bic_tab]{bic_tab()}}. Both are larger than \code{AIC(fit$opt_mod)} and
\code{BIC(fit$opt_mod)}, which leave the kernel parameters out. The \code{nobs}
attribute is the sample size those tables use, for example the event
count for Cox and conditional logistic models.
}

